---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_URL, ENTITY_STATEMENT } from '../data/site.js';

const profileSchema = {
  '@type': 'ProfilePage',
  '@id': `${SITE_URL}/about#page`,
  url: `${SITE_URL}/about`,
  name: 'About Ally Madrone',
  mainEntity: { '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#ally` }
};
---

<Base
  title="About"
  path="/about"
  description={`${ENTITY_STATEMENT} This page tells the integrated story and provides copy-paste bios in three lengths.`}
  extraSchema={profileSchema}
>
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="kicker">{ENTITY_STATEMENT}</p>

  <img
    class="headshot"
    src="/images/ally-madrone.jpg"
    alt="Ally Madrone, a smiling bearded man in an olive button-up shirt wearing a wire-wrapped stone pendant"
    width="1200"
    height="1200"
    loading="eager"
  />

  <p class="lede">
    My career looks like a zigzag until you see the thread. Clinical research, then a company I
    built and ran, then enterprise sales in identity, then product in content security. Every one
    of those is the same job: understand a system well enough to protect the people inside it, then
    build something they'll pay for that does it.
  </p>

  <p>
    I started in clinical research at Oregon Health &amp; Science University, coordinating Phase 3
    trials for five years and learning how evidence gets made and how easily it gets contaminated.
    In 2015 I founded Root Connection in Portland and grew it from zero to a million dollars in
    annual revenue in two years: fifty-plus client contracts over six years, all sold myself, a marketing engine I
    built from scratch, crews and vendors to keep on schedule and on budget, and a regulated
    consulting practice on the side. Running a company taught me the job I still do. Find the
    customer's real problem, sell the fix, and deliver it.
  </p>

  <p>
    In 2020 I moved into identity and fraud, and into sales. I was Veriff's first U.S. hire, built
    its sales development team from one person to twelve, and then beat quota for five straight
    quarters as an enterprise account executive selling identity verification to financial
    institutions. At Trust Stamp I was the first North American go-to-market hire, built a $10MM
    pipeline from a flat territory, and wrote the PRD for the deepfake detection API from scratch.
    That work put me in the room where synthetic media stopped being a curiosity and became an
    attack surface.
  </p>

  <p>
    Since January 2025 I've been VP of Product at Deep Media, a content security infrastructure
    company. I lead product across the platform, from the composable detection
    and tool layer to Skills and the Guardian investigation agents that hunt digital harms across
    the surface, deep, and dark web. Deep Media's work is trusted by the U.S. Department of Defense
    and major technology platforms.
  </p>

  <p>
    The thread that holds all of it together is a question I've carried since my first company: how do we
    act with integrity toward the systems we're entangled with, ecological, digital, human, and
    artificial. Permaculture ethics and platform trust and safety are closer than they look. Both
    are about designing conditions where the whole system can stay healthy, instead of fighting each
    symptom after the damage is done.
  </p>

  <p>
    The rest of the whole person lives on the <a href="/garden">garden</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>Bios for copy and paste</h2>
  <p>
    These are maintained by me and safe to use verbatim for event programs, introductions, and
    press. A speaker headshot is on the <a href="/speaking">speaking</a> page.
  </p>

  <div class="bio-block">
    <h3>Short bio (50 words)</h3>
    <p>
      Ally Madrone is VP of Product at Deep Media, a content security infrastructure company trusted
      by the U.S. Department of Defense and major technology platforms. He builds AI systems that
      detect synthetic media and disrupt fraud networks, and he speaks widely on AI-enabled
      financial crime.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="bio-block">
    <h3>Medium bio (150 words)</h3>
    <p>
      Ally Madrone is VP of Product at Deep Media, a content security infrastructure company trusted
      by the U.S. Department of Defense and major technology platforms. He leads product across Deep
      Media's platform, including autonomous AI agents that investigate fraud, disinformation, and
      other digital harms across the surface, deep, and dark web.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ally has spent his career at the intersection of identity, fraud, and trust and safety. He wrote the product requirements for Trust Stamp's deepfake detection API, worked enterprise identity verification at
      Veriff, and is a regular speaker for organizations including the International Association of
      Financial Crimes Investigators on deepfakes, synthetic identity fraud, and AI-enabled social
      engineering. Before tech, he founded and ran Root Connection, a horticultural research and
      design firm he grew to $1MM in annual revenue. He holds a B.S. in Horticulture from Oregon
      State University.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="bio-block">
    <h3>Long bio (300 words)</h3>
    <p>
      Ally Madrone is VP of Product at Deep Media, a content security infrastructure company trusted
      by the U.S. Department of Defense and major technology platforms. He leads product strategy
      across the full platform: a composable detection API and MCP tool layer, Skills composed from those tools, and Guardian, autonomous AI agents that proactively investigate fraud,
      disinformation, and other digital harms across the surface, deep, and dark web.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ally's path into content security ran through identity and fraud. At Trust Stamp he authored the PRD for the deepfake detection API, evaluated
      internal models against it, and packaged synthetic media detection for financial crime, KYC,
      and identity fraud use cases, and authoring technical responses to federal RFIs on
      synthetic identity detection and biometric liveness. He was Veriff's first U.S. hire, built its
      sales development team from one to twelve, and then sold enterprise identity verification to
      financial institutions, developing deep expertise in KYC workflows and the operational
      reality of fraud teams in regulated industries.
    </p>
    <p>
      He is a frequent educator on AI-enabled financial crime, speaking regularly for the
      International Association of Financial Crimes Investigators and other industry bodies on
      deepfakes, synthetic identity fraud, and AI-driven social engineering. He spoke at TrustCon
      2024 on deepfake detection, and his team FaceLock won the Safety by Design Trust &amp; Safety
      Hackathon in 2024.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before tech, Ally founded Root Connection, a scientific research and horticultural design firm
      in Portland, Oregon, and grew it to $1MM in annual revenue. He holds a B.S. in Horticulture on
      the research option from Oregon State University. The through line of his work is a single
      question: how do we act with integrity toward the systems we're entangled with, ecological,
      digital, human, and artificial.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
